"""Original Stage 07 advanced-language depth: bounded Python 3.13 sections with executed transfer practice."""
from python_guide_depth import add, task

add('lazy-generator', 'Functional HOWTO: generators and lazy pipelines with itertools.islice',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/howto/functional.html#generators', [
    "A generator function returns a generator object without executing its body. Each next() call runs the body until the following yield, hands back that value and suspends with all local variables preserved. Values are produced on demand rather than all at once.",
    "Laziness makes infinite sources practical: a generator can loop forever, and a consumer such as itertools.islice(source, n) pulls exactly the number of items it needs. Generator expressions chain onto generators without materialising intermediate lists, so a filter-and-take pipeline only does the work its output requires.",
    "Laziness also moves when work and errors happen. Side effects inside the generator occur during consumption, not at creation. A pipeline that is never consumed does no work, and an exception in a source appears where a consumer advances it. Count or log work at the source when you need to verify the amount of computation.",
    "The transfer filters even numbers from an infinite counter and takes the first few. It records how many source values were produced, which shows that the pipeline stops at the last needed item."
    ],
    ("An infinite generator feeds (x for x in gen if x % 2 == 0), and islice takes 3 items. How many source values are produced?",
     "Five: 0 through 4, stopping at the third even value.",
     ["Infinitely many, because filtering must scan the whole source.", "Three, because islice controls the source directly.", "Six, because generators prefetch one item."],
     "Each stage pulls only what the next stage requests; the third even value is 4, the fifth source value."),
    task("Transfer: take from an infinite pipeline",
         "data is a non-negative limit. numbers() yields 0, 1, 2, ... forever and records each produced value in work. Take limit even values through a generator expression and islice. Return [taken_values, len(work)].",
         "work = []\ndef numbers():\n    n = 0\n    while True:\n        work.append(n)\n        yield n\n        n += 1\nevens = (x for x in numbers() if x % 2 == 0)\nresult = [list(islice(evens, data)), len(work)]",
         [(3,[[0,2,4],5]),(0,[[],0]),(1,[[0],1])],
         ("if x % 2 == 0","if x % 2 == 1"),
         ["islice stops pulling once it has the requested count.", "The filter pulls source values until one passes.", "A limit of zero pulls nothing at all."],
         "Taking three evens produces 0, 2 and 4 after five source values. A zero limit never starts the pipeline. Selecting odd values instead would change both the output and the work count.",
         [[[0,2,4],3],[[0,2,4],6],[[0,1,2],3]],
         "from itertools import islice"),
    ("When does a generator's body run?", "Only as it is consumed: each next() resumes it to the following yield; creating it runs nothing."))

add('generator-send', 'Expressions: generator.send() and the value of a yield expression',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/reference/expressions.html#generator.send', [
    "yield is an expression. value = yield out hands out to the caller and suspends; when the caller resumes with gen.send(x), the suspended yield evaluates to x. next(gen) is equivalent to gen.send(None).",
    "A new generator has not reached any yield, so there is nowhere to deliver a value. Sending a non-None value first raises TypeError. Prime it with next(gen), which runs to the first yield and returns its output. Some code wraps priming in a helper so callers cannot forget it.",
    "A generator that both receives and yields can keep running state, such as a running mean, without a class. Each send updates state and returns the new summary. Classes or explicit objects are usually clearer for complex protocols; use send for small, well-documented state machines.",
    "The transfer primes an averager, sends several values and collects each returned running mean. The first output, from priming, is None because no value has arrived yet."
    ],
    ("Why must a fresh generator be primed with next() before send(5)?",
     "It has not paused at a yield yet, so there is no yield expression to receive the value; sending non-None raises TypeError.",
     ["send() always discards the first value.", "Priming resets the generator's local variables.", "Generators can only receive values after StopIteration."],
     "Execution must reach a yield before a sent value has a destination."),
    task("Transfer: a running-mean coroutine",
         "data is a list of numbers. averager() yields the current mean (None before any value). Prime it, send every value and return [primed_output] + [each send result].",
         "def averager():\n    total, count, average = 0, 0, None\n    while True:\n        value = yield average\n        total += value\n        count += 1\n        average = total / count\ng = averager()\nprimed = next(g)\nresult = [primed] + [g.send(x) for x in data]",
         [([10,20,30],[None,10.0,15.0,20.0]),([],[None]),([-4,4],[None,-4.0,0.0])],
         ("average = total / count","average = value / count"),
         ["next(g) runs to the first yield and returns None.", "Each send makes the suspended yield evaluate to the sent value.", "Keep total and count across iterations."],
         "Priming returns None. Sending 10, 20 and 30 gives running means 10, 15 and 20. Dividing the latest value instead of the total gives a plausible but wrong summary.",
         [[10.0,15.0,20.0],[None,10,30,60],[None,10.0,20.0,30.0]]),
    ("What does next(gen) equal in send terms?", "gen.send(None). A fresh generator must be primed this way before sending a real value."))

add('generator-close', 'Expressions: generator.close() and contextlib.closing for partial consumption',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/reference/expressions.html#generator.close', [
    "A generator suspended inside try/finally or with holds that cleanup until it either finishes or is closed. close() raises GeneratorExit at the suspended yield so the finally block runs. Closing a finished generator does nothing.",
    "Breaking out of a for loop does not close the generator. If another name still refers to it, cleanup waits until garbage collection, which on other interpreters or with reference cycles may be much later. Files, sockets or locks held by a generator can therefore stay open after the loop.",
    "contextlib.closing(gen) calls gen.close() when its with block exits, making cleanup deterministic even after break or an exception. A generator should not yield inside finally in response to GeneratorExit; that raises RuntimeError.",
    "The transfer stops early on a negative value and records the order of events. Closing happens at the end of the with block, after the loop's own follow-up step."
    ],
    ("A for loop breaks out of a generator that still has a name bound to it. When does its finally block run?",
     "Not immediately; only when the generator is closed or collected, which closing() makes deterministic.",
     ["Immediately at break, always.", "Never, because finally only runs after StopIteration.", "When the next generator is created."],
     "break leaves the generator suspended; an explicit close (or closing()) delivers GeneratorExit."),
    task("Transfer: deterministic cleanup after break",
         "data is a list of numbers. rows() records 'open', yields values and records 'close' in finally. Inside with closing(rows()), take values until a negative appears, then record 'after-loop'. Return [taken, events].",
         "events = []\ndef rows():\n    events.append('open')\n    try:\n        for x in data:\n            yield x\n    finally:\n        events.append('close')\ntaken = []\nwith closing(rows()) as source:\n    for x in source:\n        if x < 0:\n            break\n        taken.append(x)\n    events.append('after-loop')\nresult = [taken, events]",
         [([1,-1,2],[[1],['open','after-loop','close']]),([],[[],['open','close','after-loop']]),([3],[[3],['open','close','after-loop']])],
         ("with closing(rows()) as source:","with nullcontext(rows()) as source:"),
         ["After break, the generator is suspended, not finished.", "When the source is exhausted, finally runs before the loop ends.", "closing() calls close() when the with block exits."],
         "With an early break, close runs at the end of the with block, after 'after-loop'. When the input is exhausted, the generator finishes on its own, so close comes first. Without closing(), the broken-off generator would stay suspended.",
         [[[1],['open','close','after-loop']],[[1,2],['open','after-loop','close']],[[1],['open','after-loop']]],
         "from contextlib import closing, nullcontext"),
    ("How do you guarantee a partially consumed generator releases its resources?", "Close it explicitly, e.g. with contextlib.closing(gen), rather than relying on garbage collection after break."))

add('yield-from', 'Expressions: yield from delegation and subgenerator return values',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/reference/expressions.html#yield-expressions', [
    "yield from sub delegates to a subiterator: every value it produces passes straight to the caller, and send() and throw() are forwarded too. It replaces a hand-written for loop and handles those forwarding details correctly.",
    "A generator's return value travels in StopIteration.value. The expression yield from sub evaluates to that value, so an outer generator can collect results from its delegates, such as how many items each chunk produced, while still streaming the items themselves.",
    "A plain for loop over a generator discards its return value. When you need the final result of a whole generator outside, catch StopIteration from next() or use yield from inside another generator. Keep returned values small summaries; the stream is the main output.",
    "The transfer streams items from several groups and collects each group's returned size, including an empty group."
    ],
    ("In size = yield from chunk(items), where does size come from?",
     "From chunk's return statement, delivered through StopIteration.value when the subgenerator finishes.",
     ["From the last value chunk yields.", "From the number of times next() was called on the outer generator.", "yield from always evaluates to None."],
     "The yield from expression's value is the subgenerator's return value."),
    task("Transfer: stream items and collect returned sizes",
         "data is a list of lists. chunk(items) yields each item and returns len(items). combined() delegates to chunk for each group with yield from and returns the list of sizes. Drive it with next() and return [streamed_values, returned_sizes].",
         "def chunk(items):\n    for x in items:\n        yield x\n    return len(items)\ndef combined():\n    sizes = []\n    for group in data:\n        size = yield from chunk(group)\n        sizes.append(size)\n    return sizes\ngen = combined()\nvalues = []\ntry:\n    while True:\n        values.append(next(gen))\nexcept StopIteration as stop:\n    sizes = stop.value\nresult = [values, sizes]",
         [([[1,2],[],[3]],[[1,2,3],[2,0,1]]),([],[[],[]]),([['a']],[['a'],[1]])],
         ("return len(items)","return None"),
         ["yield from passes each item to the outer caller.", "The value of the yield from expression is chunk's return value.", "The outer return value arrives in StopIteration.value."],
         "The items stream as 1, 2, 3 while the sizes 2, 0 and 1 come back through the delegation. Without chunk's return, every collected size would be None.",
         [[[1,2,3],[None,None,None]],[[[1,2],[],[3]],[2,0,1]],[[1,2,3],[3]]]),
    ("What does a for loop lose that yield from keeps?", "The subgenerator's return value; yield from evaluates to it (StopIteration.value)."))

add('decorator-wraps', 'functools.wraps and parameterized decorators',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/functools.html#functools.wraps', [
    "A decorator is a callable that takes a function and returns a replacement. A parameterized decorator such as @retry(3) is a function that returns the actual decorator, so there are three layers: the configuration, the decorator and the wrapper.",
    "Without functools.wraps the wrapper's __name__, __doc__ and module replace the original's, which confuses logs, debuggers and documentation tools. wraps copies them and records the original as __wrapped__, which inspect and tests can use to reach the undecorated function.",
    "A retry decorator must decide which exceptions are retryable, how many attempts to make and what happens when attempts run out. Retrying every exception hides bugs; re-raising the last error on exhaustion preserves the failure. Real network retries also need delays and idempotent operations.",
    "The transfer counts calls to a function that fails a set number of times, checks the attempt on which it succeeded and verifies the preserved metadata."
    ],
    ("Why does @retry(3) need three nested functions?",
     "retry(3) returns a decorator; the decorator receives the function and returns the wrapper that runs at each call.",
     ["Python requires every decorator to have three layers.", "The third layer is needed only for functools.wraps.", "Each layer represents one retry attempt."],
     "The outer call captures configuration; the decorator captures the function; the wrapper implements each call."),
    task("Transfer: a parameterized retry decorator",
         "data is [failures, limit]. flaky() raises ValueError on its first `failures` calls and then returns 'ok'. @retry(limit) retries ValueError up to limit attempts and returns [value, attempt], re-raising on exhaustion. Return [outcome or 'gave-up', call_count, flaky.__name__, flaky.__wrapped__.__name__].",
         "def retry(times):\n    def decorate(fn):\n        @functools.wraps(fn)\n        def wrapper(*args):\n            for attempt in range(1, times + 1):\n                try:\n                    return [fn(*args), attempt]\n                except ValueError:\n                    if attempt == times:\n                        raise\n        return wrapper\n    return decorate\nfailures, limit = data\ncalls = []\n@retry(limit)\ndef flaky():\n    calls.append(1)\n    if len(calls) <= failures:\n        raise ValueError('transient')\n    return 'ok'\ntry:\n    outcome = flaky()\nexcept ValueError:\n    outcome = 'gave-up'\nresult = [outcome, len(calls), flaky.__name__, flaky.__wrapped__.__name__]",
         [([1,3],[['ok',2],2,'flaky','flaky']),([0,1],[['ok',1],1,'flaky','flaky']),([3,3],['gave-up',3,'flaky','flaky'])],
         ("for attempt in range(1, times + 1):","for attempt in range(1, times):"),
         ["Attempts are numbered 1 through times inclusive.", "Re-raise only after the final attempt fails.", "functools.wraps provides both __name__ and __wrapped__."],
         "One failure then success returns on attempt 2 after two calls. Three failures with three attempts exhaust the retries and re-raise. An off-by-one range drops the last attempt and silently returns None.",
         [[['ok',1],2,'flaky','flaky'],[['ok',2],2,'wrapper','flaky'],['gave-up',3,'flaky','flaky']],
         "import functools"),
    ("What does functools.wraps add besides __name__?", "It copies __doc__, __module__, __qualname__ and more, and sets __wrapped__ to the original function."))

add('cache-purity', 'functools.lru_cache: eviction, cache_info and hashable arguments',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/functools.html#functools.lru_cache', [
    "lru_cache stores results keyed by the call's arguments. A repeated call with equal, hashable arguments returns the stored result without running the function. Unhashable arguments such as lists raise TypeError, because they cannot be dictionary keys.",
    "With maxsize set, the cache keeps at most that many entries and evicts the least recently used one when full. A hit refreshes an entry's recency. cache_info() reports hits, misses, maxsize and current size, which lets tests confirm caching behavior instead of guessing.",
    "Caching is only correct for functions whose result depends solely on their arguments and that have no needed side effects. Reading files, the clock or mutable globals breaks that assumption; call cache_clear() when the underlying data changes. Cached return values are shared, so do not mutate a returned list.",
    "The transfer traces an LRU cache of size two over a call sequence where a hit protects an entry from eviction."
    ],
    ("With lru_cache(maxsize=2), calls go f(1), f(2), f(1), f(3). Which entry is evicted by f(3)?",
     "f(2), because the hit on f(1) made 2 the least recently used.",
     ["f(1), because it was inserted first.", "Neither; maxsize counts misses, not entries.", "f(3) is not cached because the cache is full."],
     "LRU eviction tracks recency of use, including hits, not insertion order."),
    task("Transfer: trace an LRU cache",
         "data is a list of integers passed to cost(n) decorated with lru_cache(maxsize=2). Afterwards, try cost([1]). Return [hits, misses, currsize, list_argument_rejected].",
         "@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=2)\ndef cost(n):\n    return n * n\nfor n in data:\n    cost(n)\ninfo = cost.cache_info()\ntry:\n    cost([1])\n    unhashable = False\nexcept TypeError:\n    unhashable = True\nresult = [info.hits, info.misses, info.currsize, unhashable]",
         [([1,2,1,3,1],[2,3,2,True]),([],[0,0,0,True]),([5,5,5],[2,1,1,True])],
         ("maxsize=2","maxsize=1"),
         ["A hit refreshes that entry's recency.", "When full, a miss evicts the least recently used entry.", "Lists cannot be cache keys."],
         "1 and 2 miss, 1 hits, 3 misses and evicts 2, and the final 1 hits: 2 hits, 3 misses, size 2. A one-entry cache would turn every alternating call into a miss.",
         [[3,2,2,True],[2,3,3,True],[2,3,2,False]],
         "import functools"),
    ("When is lru_cache unsafe?", "When results depend on anything besides the hashable arguments (files, time, globals) or callers mutate the shared cached return value."))

add('pattern-match', 'Compound statements: match with sequence, class, mapping patterns and guards',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/reference/compound_stmts.html#the-match-statement', [
    "match compares a subject against case patterns from top to bottom and runs the first that matches. Sequence patterns such as ['move', x, y] check length and items; mapping patterns such as {'quit': True} check that keys exist with matching values, ignoring extra keys.",
    "Class patterns such as int(x) check the type and bind the value. A guard, if condition, adds a test that runs after binding; if it fails, matching continues with later cases. Order matters: put specific cases before general fallbacks such as ['move', *_].",
    "Strings are deliberately not treated as sequences by sequence patterns, so 'say hi' does not match ['say', text]. The wildcard _ matches anything and binds nothing. A final case _ makes unsupported shapes explicit instead of leaving a silent fall-through.",
    "The transfer dispatches command-like data with type checks, a guard for non-negative coordinates, a mapping pattern and a string that must not be mistaken for a sequence."
    ],
    ("Does the subject 'say hi' (a str) match case ['say', str(text)]?",
     "No; sequence patterns never match str, bytes or bytearray subjects.",
     ["Yes; the string is split on whitespace.", "Yes; strings are sequences of characters.", "It raises TypeError."],
     "Python excludes text and bytes from sequence patterns to avoid accidental character-wise matching."),
    task("Transfer: dispatch structured commands",
         "data is a list of commands. Return handle(c) for each: ['move', int x, int y] with both >= 0 → 'move x,y'; any other move list → 'bad move'; ['say', str text] → 'say text'; a mapping with quit True → 'quit'; anything else → 'unknown'.",
         "def handle(command):\n    match command:\n        case ['move', int(x), int(y)] if x >= 0 and y >= 0:\n            return f'move {x},{y}'\n        case ['move', *_]:\n            return 'bad move'\n        case ['say', str(text)]:\n            return 'say ' + text\n        case {'quit': True}:\n            return 'quit'\n        case _:\n            return 'unknown'\nresult = [handle(c) for c in data]",
         [([['move',1,2],['move',-1,0],['say','hi'],{'quit':True},['jump']],['move 1,2','bad move','say hi','quit','unknown']),([],[]),([['move',1],{'quit':False},'say hi'],['bad move','unknown','unknown'])],
         ("case ['move', int(x), int(y)] if x >= 0 and y >= 0:","case ['move', int(x), int(y)]:"),
         ["A failed guard lets matching continue to later cases.", "Mapping patterns ignore extra keys but require matching values.", "A plain string never matches a sequence pattern."],
         "The negative move fails the guard and falls to 'bad move'. quit False does not match {'quit': True}. The bare string 'say hi' is not a sequence subject, so it is unknown. Removing the guard would accept negative coordinates.",
         [['move 1,2','move -1,0','say hi','quit','unknown'],['move 1,2','bad move','say hi','unknown','unknown'],['move 1,2','bad move','unknown','quit','unknown']]),
    ("What happens when a case guard is false?", "That case does not match and matching continues with the next case."))

add('typing-runtime', 'typing: get_type_hints and explicit runtime validation at boundaries',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/typing.html#typing.get_type_hints', [
    "Annotations are metadata. The interpreter stores them but does not check calls against them: a function annotated value: int happily receives a string. Static checkers such as mypy or pyright use annotations before the program runs.",
    "When untrusted data enters at a boundary, such as a request, file or configuration, validate it explicitly at runtime. typing.get_type_hints(fn) resolves a function's annotations into objects your validation code can read. Validation libraries build on the same idea.",
    "Decide precise rules. bool is a subclass of int, so isinstance(True, int) is True; an exact type check rejects it. By the numeric-tower convention, a float parameter usually accepts int too. Write these choices down and test them, because the annotation alone does not express them.",
    "The transfer validates keyword arguments against resolved hints and then calls the function unchecked once to show that annotations alone enforce nothing."
    ],
    ("A function is annotated def f(x: int) -> int and is called as f('a'). What happens at runtime?",
     "It runs with 'a'; annotations are not enforced by the interpreter.",
     ["TypeError at the call site.", "The string is converted to an int.", "A warning is printed and None is returned."],
     "Static checkers report the mismatch; runtime validation must be written explicitly."),
    task("Transfer: validate against resolved hints",
         "data is a list of [value, factor] pairs for scale(value: int, factor: float). checked() uses get_type_hints: value must be exactly int (bool rejected); factor accepts int or float but not bool. Return each checked result or 'rejected <name>', followed by the unchecked scale('ab', 2).",
         "def scale(value: int, factor: float) -> float:\n    return value * factor\nhints = get_type_hints(scale)\ndef checked(**kwargs):\n    for name, value in kwargs.items():\n        expected = hints[name]\n        if expected is float:\n            ok = isinstance(value, (int, float)) and not isinstance(value, bool)\n        else:\n            ok = type(value) is expected\n        if not ok:\n            return 'rejected ' + name\n    return scale(**kwargs)\nresult = [checked(value=v, factor=f) for v, f in data] + [scale('ab', 2)]",
         [([[2,1.5],[True,2.0],[3,'x']],[3.0,'rejected value','rejected factor','abab']),([],['abab']),([[4,2]],[8,'abab'])],
         ("ok = type(value) is expected","ok = isinstance(value, expected)"),
         ["get_type_hints returns a name-to-type dictionary.", "bool passes isinstance(x, int); an exact type check rejects it.", "The unchecked call shows annotations do not stop 'ab' * 2."],
         "2 * 1.5 is 3.0. True is rejected by the exact int check, and 'x' is not numeric. The direct call returns 'abab' because nothing enforced the annotation. isinstance would let True through as an int.",
         [[3.0,2.0,'rejected factor','abab'],[3.0,'rejected value','rejected factor','TypeError'],[3.0,'rejected value',None,'abab']],
         "from typing import get_type_hints"),
    ("Do annotations validate calls at runtime?", "No. Use static checkers, and validate untrusted boundary data explicitly (e.g. via get_type_hints)."))

add('protocol-shape', 'typing.Protocol and the limits of runtime_checkable isinstance checks',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/typing.html#typing.runtime_checkable', [
    "A Protocol describes the members an object must have, without requiring inheritance. A static checker verifies that a passed object structurally matches, including method signatures. This is the typed form of duck typing.",
    "@runtime_checkable lets isinstance(obj, Proto) run at runtime, but it only checks that the named members exist. It does not check that they are callable with the right signature, their types or behavior. An object with close = 3 passes an isinstance check for a protocol requiring close().",
    "Use runtime protocol checks for coarse routing, then handle failures when actually calling. For trustworthy guarantees, rely on static checking plus tests. Runtime protocol checks are also slower than ordinary isinstance against a concrete class.",
    "The transfer routes three objects through a runtime protocol check: a real implementation, an attribute that is not callable and an object lacking the member."
    ],
    ("An object has close = 3. Does isinstance(obj, Closer) pass for a runtime_checkable Protocol requiring def close(self)?",
     "Yes; the runtime check only verifies the member exists, not that it is a callable with the right signature.",
     ["No; runtime_checkable verifies signatures.", "No; non-method attributes are always rejected.", "It raises TypeError because 3 is not a function."],
     "Runtime protocol checks are shallow presence checks; call-time handling or static checking must cover the rest."),
    task("Transfer: shallow runtime protocol routing",
         "data is a list of kinds: 'file' (real close method), 'liar' (close = 3) or 'none' (no close). safe_close returns 'skipped' if the object fails isinstance(obj, Closer), 'closed' on a successful call, or 'not-callable' on TypeError. Return the results.",
         "@runtime_checkable\nclass Closer(Protocol):\n    def close(self) -> None: ...\nclass File:\n    def close(self):\n        return None\nclass Liar:\n    close = 3\nclass Nothing:\n    pass\ndef safe_close(obj):\n    if not isinstance(obj, Closer):\n        return 'skipped'\n    try:\n        obj.close()\n        return 'closed'\n    except TypeError:\n        return 'not-callable'\nkinds = {'file': File, 'liar': Liar, 'none': Nothing}\nresult = [safe_close(kinds[k]()) for k in data]",
         [(['file','liar','none'],['closed','not-callable','skipped']),([],[]),(['none','file'],['skipped','closed'])],
         ("if not isinstance(obj, Closer):","if not hasattr(obj, 'open'):"),
         ["The runtime check only tests that close exists.", "Calling the integer attribute raises TypeError.", "An object without close is skipped before any call."],
         "File closes. Liar passes the shallow check but fails when called. Nothing lacks the member and is skipped. Checking the wrong attribute name would skip everything.",
         [['closed','skipped','skipped'],['closed','closed','skipped'],['closed','not-callable','closed']],
         "from typing import Protocol, runtime_checkable"),
    ("What does isinstance check for a runtime_checkable Protocol?", "Only that the required members exist, not their signatures, types or behavior."))

add('metaclass-creation', 'Data model: __init_subclass__ as the simpler class-creation hook',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/reference/datamodel.html#object.__init_subclass__', [
    "A metaclass controls how class objects are created, but most class-creation needs are simpler. __init_subclass__ runs on the parent whenever a subclass is defined, and receives keyword arguments written in the class statement, such as class CSV(Plugin, name='csv').",
    "This is enough for plugin registries, enforcing required class attributes or validating subclass configuration. It composes with inheritance and avoids metaclass conflicts when a class has bases with different metaclasses. Call super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs) so cooperative hooks keep working.",
    "Registries are shared mutable state. Decide what a duplicate name means, here a ValueError, and how tests reset the registry. Because registration happens at class definition, importing a module has side effects, which is worth documenting.",
    "The transfer defines subclasses dynamically with type(name, bases, namespace, **kwargs), which passes the keywords on to __init_subclass__, and checks duplicate rejection."
    ],
    ("Which hook is usually preferable to a custom metaclass for registering subclasses?",
     "__init_subclass__ on the base class, which runs for each subclass definition.",
     ["__new__ on every subclass.", "__set_name__ on the base class.", "__class_getitem__."],
     "__init_subclass__ covers most per-subclass customization without metaclass complexity or conflicts."),
    task("Transfer: a plugin registry without a metaclass",
         "data is a list of plugin names. Create a subclass of Plugin for each with type('P', (Plugin,), {}, name=name). __init_subclass__ registers the class under its name and raises ValueError for duplicates. Return [created_names_with_'duplicate'_markers, sorted_registry_names].",
         "class Plugin:\n    registry = {}\n    def __init_subclass__(cls, *, name, **kwargs):\n        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)\n        if name in Plugin.registry:\n            raise ValueError(name)\n        Plugin.registry[name] = cls\ncreated = []\nfor name in data:\n    try:\n        type('P', (Plugin,), {}, name=name)\n        created.append(name)\n    except ValueError:\n        created.append('duplicate')\nresult = [created, sorted(Plugin.registry)]",
         [(['csv','json','csv'],[['csv','json','duplicate'],['csv','json']]),([],[[],[]]),(['x'],[['x'],['x']])],
         ("Plugin.registry[name] = cls","Plugin.registry = {name: cls}"),
         ["Class keywords are passed to __init_subclass__.", "Insert into the shared registry; do not replace it.", "A duplicate raises before the registry changes."],
         "csv and json register; the second csv raises ValueError. Replacing the dictionary instead of inserting would forget earlier plugins and let the duplicate through.",
         [[['csv','json','csv'],['csv','json']],[['csv','json','duplicate'],['csv','csv','json']],[['csv','json','duplicate'],['csv']]]),
    ("What does __init_subclass__ receive from class P(Base, name='x')?", "The new subclass as cls and name='x' as a keyword argument; forward remaining kwargs to super()."))

add('weak-ownership', 'weakref.WeakValueDictionary: caches that do not keep values alive',
    'https://docs.python.org/3.13/library/weakref.html#weakref.WeakValueDictionary', [
    "A normal dictionary holds strong references, so a cache built on dict keeps every value alive for as long as the cache exists. That is a common source of unbounded memory growth in long-running services and notebooks.",
    "WeakValueDictionary holds weak references to its values. When the last strong reference elsewhere disappears, the entry is removed automatically. The cache can then share an object while someone uses it, without becoming its owner.",
    "On CPython, objects without reference cycles are freed as soon as their reference count drops to zero, so entries vanish immediately. Other interpreters, free-threaded builds or objects in cycles may delay collection until the garbage collector runs; tests that observe removal should state that dependency. Some built-in types, such as int and list, cannot be weakly referenced.",
    "The transfer creates objects, keeps strong references to only some of them and observes which cache entries survive after the temporary references go away."
    ],
    ("What keeps an entry alive in a WeakValueDictionary?",
     "A strong reference to the value held somewhere else; the dictionary's own reference does not count.",
     ["Accessing the key at least once.", "The dictionary itself until clear() is called.", "Nothing; entries expire after a timeout."],
     "Weak references observe without owning; the value lives as long as some other strong reference exists."),
    task("Transfer: a non-owning cache",
         "data is a list of [name, keep]. Create a Model for each, store it in a WeakValueDictionary and append it to held only if keep is true. Drop the loop variable's reference, then return the sorted cache keys (CPython reference counting).",
         "class Model:\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\ncache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()\nheld = []\nmodel = None\nfor name, keep in data:\n    model = Model(name)\n    cache[name] = model\n    if keep:\n        held.append(model)\nmodel = None\nresult = sorted(cache.keys())",
         [([['a',True],['b',False],['c',True]],['a','c']),([],[]),([['x',False]],[])],
         ("cache = weakref.WeakValueDictionary()","cache = {}"),
         ["Only held and the loop variable hold strong references.", "Rebinding model drops the previous temporary reference.", "A plain dict would own every value."],
         "a and c stay alive through held; b loses its last strong reference when the loop variable is rebound, so its entry disappears. A plain dict would keep all three.",
         [['a','b','c'],['b'],[]],
         "import weakref"),
    ("Why use WeakValueDictionary for a cache?", "It shares live objects without owning them, so entries vanish when callers drop their last strong reference."))
